The conversation began with James requesting help to format a dataset for a report that contained numerous columns. I suggested not including all eight scenario columns in a single table due to readability issues, proposing instead to create a core projections table and isolate sparse data into separate tables. He clarified that one of his main messages was to emphasize that lower warming levels could reduce future damages. I recommended displaying the data in two panels organized by warming levels for 2050 and 2100, which contrasted outcomes. James later extracted the data into two tables and requested formatting, including the use of the `gt` package in R. During the discussion, we addressed errors related to grouping and deprecated functions, concluding with recommendations on using `sub_missing()` instead of `fmt_missing()` and adjustments to the table formatting process.
